# ---- Required ----

# MongoDB connection string.
# Local:  mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017
# Atlas:  mongodb+srv://USER:PASSWORD@cluster0.xxxxx.mongodb.net
MONGODB_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017

# Database name
MONGODB_DB=chatbot_forge

# Secret used to encrypt each creator's provider API key at rest (AES-256-GCM).
# Generate one with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# Changing this makes every previously saved API key unreadable.
ENCRYPTION_SECRET=replace-me-with-a-long-random-string

# ---- Optional ----

# Public base URL, used to generate share links and embed snippets.
# Leave unset in dev and it falls back to the request's own origin.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://your-domain.com

# Name of an Atlas Vector Search index on the `chunks` collection. When set, the
# knowledge base uses $vectorSearch; when unset, similarity is computed in the
# app, which is fine into the low thousands of chunks and works on local mongod.
# See the "Vector search on Atlas" section of the README for the index JSON.
# MONGODB_VECTOR_INDEX=chunks_vector_index

# Netlify caps request bodies at 6MB and base64-encodes binary uploads, which
# leaves about 4MB of real file. netlify.toml sets this to 4 for that reason.
# Raise it on a host with a larger body limit. Defaults to 20.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MAX_UPLOAD_MB=20

# How long ingestion may spend fetching before it stops and indexes what it has.
# Keep it below the host's function timeout (60s on Netlify, not raisable).
# Defaults to 240000 (4 minutes), which suits a self-hosted Node server.
# INGEST_BUDGET_MS=240000

# The ROOT admin: this email's account always passes the admin check, opens
# /admin, and cannot be demoted from the panel. It exists to bootstrap the
# platform (someone has to appoint the first admin) and as the way back in if
# every other admin is demoted by mistake.
#
# Everyone else is promoted from the Users table on /admin, which stores
# role: 'admin' on the account. Leave this unset and nobody is an admin at all,
# so the panel is unreachable.
# ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Platform keys for the paid tier are NOT environment variables any more.
#
# An admin sets them on /admin ("Platform keys"), and they are stored encrypted
# in the database under ENCRYPTION_SECRET. A bot saved with a BLANK key runs on
# them — but only when its owner is a registered account with credits left, and
# only on the models listed in src/lib/platform.ts. One message = one credit.
#
# OPENAI_API_KEY and friends are no longer read by anything; delete them from
# your deploy once the keys are entered in the panel.

# ---- Email (required for password resets) ----

# Without these the app still runs, but nobody can recover a forgotten
# password: the reset link is written to the server log instead of being sent.
#
# Any SMTP mailbox will do — a Google Workspace account, Fastmail, your host's
# relay, or the SMTP endpoint a transactional provider gives you. Only SMTP_HOST
# and MAIL_FROM are required; user and pass can be left out for a relay that
# does not authenticate.
#
# Port 587 (STARTTLS) is the default and is the one to use on Netlify: the
# Lambda that functions run on blocks outbound port 25. Use 465 for implicit
# TLS. Certificates are always verified.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=you@your-domain.com
# SMTP_PASS=an-app-password-not-your-login-password
# SMTP_SECURE=false
# MAIL_FROM=Chatbot Forge <hello@your-domain.com>

# ---- Payments (optional) ----

# With these set, /account grows a "buy credits" section backed by Stripe
# Checkout. Without them the buttons stay hidden and top-ups remain manual
# through /admin, which is how the app worked before payments existed.
#
# The webhook secret is not optional if the key is set: credits are granted by
# the webhook and by nothing else, and an unsigned webhook is refused.
# Point a Stripe endpoint at https://your-domain.com/api/billing/webhook and
# subscribe it to `checkout.session.completed`.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_live_...
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CURRENCY=usd

# ---- Legal pages ----

# /privacy and /terms describe what this software actually does, but they carry
# bracketed placeholders until these are set. Have both reviewed before taking
# paying customers.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_OPERATOR_NAME=Your Company Ltd
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIVACY_EMAIL=privacy@your-domain.com
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_EMAIL=support@your-domain.com

# ---- Data retention ----

# How long a booking request — a visitor's name and contact details — is kept
# before a TTL index deletes it. Defaults to 365 days. Conversation transcripts
# are per-bot and set in the builder.
# BOOKING_RETENTION_DAYS=365

# ---- Self-hosting escape hatch ----

# Lets a bot point at a private address (http://localhost:11434/v1 for Ollama,
# or a box on your LAN). OFF by default: on a hosted deployment, a bot aimed at
# an internal address is only ever an attack.
#
# It applies to model and embedding endpoints only. The knowledge-base crawler
# always refuses private addresses, because that path reads a page and hands
# the text back to whoever is chatting.
# ALLOW_PRIVATE_ENDPOINTS=1
